Dear 3D Printing Industry Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.3 has been released. It is not an industrial PLM platform or a
slicer; it is local desktop software for the preparation gap between imported
geometry and an external polymer-AM slicing workflow.

The defining capability of the current version is feature-level editing on
geometry that has no design history. Resizing a recognised feature has been
possible for a while; moving one is what was missing:

* a recognised hole, peg, countersink or dome in imported STL or 3MF geometry
  can be moved, rotated, resized, duplicated and removed, with its original
  axis preserved and its feature identity retained, so dependent fits keep
  their reference;
* the measured values appear as editable fields with a live preview, and one
  option applies an edit to all features of the same kind as a single
  transaction with a single undo;
* recognition is bounded by manufacturability: features below half a
  millimetre are discarded, which removed 296 of 1130 entries on a sample part.

Every geometry change remains a parameterised, reproducible operation. An
optional language model may invoke the same registered API as the UI, but it
cannot calculate geometry or execute generated source. Editing, analysis and
export remain available without AI, an account or an internet connection.

Since the first public 0.1 demo, the release history records 484 curated
user-facing changes. Solidon is packaged for Windows, macOS and Linux and
supports a file-based activation route for workstations that remain offline.

Product: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Release history: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide the released build, a technical fact sheet, a sample STL-to-3MF
workflow and the resulting slicer handoff data for editorial evaluation.

